+| D-240 | 2026-11-11 | delivery-orchestrator | **Rounds 5 and 6 of the DG6 re-gate on candidate 5e819f7a: every product finding closed; a re-freeze is needed only so that the record fix of F-DG6-911/951 lies inside a frozen candidate.**<br>- **Round 5:** code-security PASS; F-DG6-990 CLOSED_VERIFIED by domain and code-security. Domain FAIL on **F-DG6-911** (Medium); the qa run was killed by a container restart (exit 143, no record) and its committed sidecar raised the same defect as **F-DG6-951**: `stages.json` went VERIFYING -> REVIEWING at the round-5 freeze with no FIXING entry for the round-4 audit BLOCK, so `--historical` exited 1. My error.<br>- **Fix `e7cb240e7`** (`stages.json` only): a FIXING entry at 06:42:33Z noted as recorded late. `--historical` DG1-DG5 exit 0.<br>- **Round 6** (same candidate): domain PASS and qa PASS; 911, 951 and (by qa) 990 verified closed. qa raised **F-DG6-952** (Low) as an observation: the history timestamps of two APPROVED gates are out of order (DG5 VERIFYING 12:00:00Z before APPROVED 06:30:00Z; DG0 REVIEWING 15:45:00Z before FIXING 15:16:00Z) and the validator does not check order. **Ruling:** I do not rewrite the history of approved gates; 952 is proposed for acceptance as an observation, which only the release-auditor can concur with.<br>- **Why a new candidate.** The closure rule (D-041/D-042) requires a fix commit to be an ancestor of the verifying round's frozen `source_commit` and of the gate's, and one candidate id has one manifest with one `source_commit` (3c8b9a3, before `e7cb240e7`). A record-only fix made after a freeze can therefore never close on that candidate. This entry changes `decisions.md`, which is in the candidate, so DG6 is re-frozen at a commit that contains `e7cb240e7`. The product tree is unchanged (equal to `60787d89f`).<br>- **Next:** round 7, all three reviewers on the new candidate, each verifying 911/951 there; then the release audit in two parts (D-140 option 1): acceptance of 952, then the gate